{"authored" [{"question" "q-64958" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{3}$ is a solution to the equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}-3y=0$ for $x>0.$ Use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(x)$ to the equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/state.json" "file_sha256" "316363ca62e5b9b31b38ab6ac6b793a7f83825a47fce0a851db42445f2380dc6" "basis" 1987 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-64958" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x)\\cdot {x}^{3}.$ \n\nDifferentiating ${y}_{2}=u(x){x}^{3}$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{x}^{3}+3u{x}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{x}^{3}+6{u}^{′}{x}^{2}+6ux.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}({u}^{″}{x}^{3}+6{u}^{′}{x}^{2}+6ux)-x({u}^{′}{x}^{3}+3u{x}^{2})-3u{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}({u}^{″}{x}^{2}+6{u}^{′}x+6u-{u}^{′}x-3u-3u) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{4}({u}^{″}x+5{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}^{4}\\ne 0$ for $x>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}x+5{u}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}x+5w=0.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}{x}^{-5}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}{x}^{-5}\\,⟹\\,u=-\\frac{1}{4}{c}_{1}{x}^{-4}+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u{x}^{3}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{3}(-\\frac{1}{4}{c}_{1}{x}^{-4}+{c}_{2}).\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${c}_{1}=-4$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/state.json" "file_sha256" "316363ca62e5b9b31b38ab6ac6b793a7f83825a47fce0a851db42445f2380dc6" "basis" 1987 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-64958" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "51af07cbd1eec904ac5c54f9efbb45b2aab327adfa8de72c66bed543937ce6fb" "basis" 1987 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-64958" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/state.json" "file_sha256" "316363ca62e5b9b31b38ab6ac6b793a7f83825a47fce0a851db42445f2380dc6" "basis" 1987 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-65000" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{2}$ is a solution to the equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-5x{y}^{′}+8y=0$ for $x>0.$ Use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(x)$ to the equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/state.json" "file_sha256" "316363ca62e5b9b31b38ab6ac6b793a7f83825a47fce0a851db42445f2380dc6" "basis" 1987 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-65000" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x)\\cdot {x}^{2}.$ \n\nDifferentiating ${y}_{2}=u(x){x}^{2}$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{x}^{2}+2ux \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{x}^{2}+4{u}^{′}x+2u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}({u}^{″}{x}^{2}+4{u}^{′}x+2u)-5x({u}^{′}{x}^{2}+2ux)+8u{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}({u}^{″}{x}^{2}+4{u}^{′}x+2u-5{u}^{′}x-10u+8u) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}({u}^{″}{x}^{2}-{u}^{′}x) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}({u}^{″}x-{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}^{3}\\ne 0$ for $x>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}x-{u}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}x-w=0.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}x.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}x\\,⟹\\,u=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{2}{x}^{2}+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u{x}^{2}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{2}(\\frac{{c}_{1}}{2}{x}^{2}+{c}_{2}).\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${c}_{1}=2$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{2}={x}^{4}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/state.json" "file_sha256" "316363ca62e5b9b31b38ab6ac6b793a7f83825a47fce0a851db42445f2380dc6" "basis" 1987 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-65000" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "51af07cbd1eec904ac5c54f9efbb45b2aab327adfa8de72c66bed543937ce6fb" "basis" 1987 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-65000" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/state.json" "file_sha256" "316363ca62e5b9b31b38ab6ac6b793a7f83825a47fce0a851db42445f2380dc6" "basis" 1987 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-65000" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}_{2}(x)=\\frac{1}{x}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/state.json" "file_sha256" "316363ca62e5b9b31b38ab6ac6b793a7f83825a47fce0a851db42445f2380dc6" "basis" 1987 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-65000" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}_{2}(x)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/state.json" "file_sha256" "316363ca62e5b9b31b38ab6ac6b793a7f83825a47fce0a851db42445f2380dc6" "basis" 1987 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-65000" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/state.json" "file_sha256" "316363ca62e5b9b31b38ab6ac6b793a7f83825a47fce0a851db42445f2380dc6" "basis" 1987 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-65000" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{3}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/state.json" "file_sha256" "316363ca62e5b9b31b38ab6ac6b793a7f83825a47fce0a851db42445f2380dc6" "basis" 1987 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-65000" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{4}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/state.json" "file_sha256" "316363ca62e5b9b31b38ab6ac6b793a7f83825a47fce0a851db42445f2380dc6" "basis" 1987 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-65000" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{4}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/state.json" "file_sha256" "316363ca62e5b9b31b38ab6ac6b793a7f83825a47fce0a851db42445f2380dc6" "basis" 1987 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-64998" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x$ is a solution to the equation ${y}^{″}+9y=0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(x)$ to the equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/state.json" "file_sha256" "316363ca62e5b9b31b38ab6ac6b793a7f83825a47fce0a851db42445f2380dc6" "basis" 1987 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-64998" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+3u\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+6{u}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-9u\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({u}^{″}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+6{u}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-9u\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)+9u\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+6{u}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″} & =-6{u}^{′}\\operatorname{cot}⁡3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}=-6w\\operatorname{cot}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}{csc}^{2}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}{csc}^{2}⁡3x\\,⟹\\,u=-\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3}\\operatorname{cot}⁡3x+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x(-\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3}\\operatorname{cot}⁡3x+{c}_{2}).\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${c}_{1}=-3$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x(\\operatorname{cot}⁡3x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/state.json" "file_sha256" "316363ca62e5b9b31b38ab6ac6b793a7f83825a47fce0a851db42445f2380dc6" "basis" 1987 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-64998" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "51af07cbd1eec904ac5c54f9efbb45b2aab327adfa8de72c66bed543937ce6fb" "basis" 1987 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-64998" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/state.json" "file_sha256" "316363ca62e5b9b31b38ab6ac6b793a7f83825a47fce0a851db42445f2380dc6" "basis" 1987 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-64998" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/state.json" "file_sha256" "316363ca62e5b9b31b38ab6ac6b793a7f83825a47fce0a851db42445f2380dc6" "basis" 1987 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-64998" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{csc}⁡3x"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/state.json" "file_sha256" "316363ca62e5b9b31b38ab6ac6b793a7f83825a47fce0a851db42445f2380dc6" "basis" 1987 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-64998" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡3x"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/state.json" "file_sha256" "316363ca62e5b9b31b38ab6ac6b793a7f83825a47fce0a851db42445f2380dc6" "basis" 1987 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-64998" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}_{2}(x)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/state.json" "file_sha256" "316363ca62e5b9b31b38ab6ac6b793a7f83825a47fce0a851db42445f2380dc6" "basis" 1987 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-64998" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}_{2}(x)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/state.json" "file_sha256" "316363ca62e5b9b31b38ab6ac6b793a7f83825a47fce0a851db42445f2380dc6" "basis" 1987 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-64998" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/state.json" "file_sha256" "316363ca62e5b9b31b38ab6ac6b793a7f83825a47fce0a851db42445f2380dc6" "basis" 1987 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-6574" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function $y_1(x) = e^{3x}$ is a solution to the equation $y'' - 6y' + 9y = 0.$ If $y_{2} = u(x) y_1(x)$ is a second solution of the equation, then which of the following equations must be satisfied by $u(x)?$\n\n- $u''=0$\n\n- $e^{3x}u'' + u=0$\n\n- $u''-u=0$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/example-6574.json" "file_sha256" "eca9c9e60be6c7ad4182da3210911b57effcb3382d3aa079eaa492a02835a915" "basis" 1987 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-6574" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =u(x){y}_{1}(x) \\\\  & =u(x){e}^{3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & =u{e}^{3x} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+3u{e}^{3x} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{e}^{3x}+6{u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+9u{e}^{3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}-6{y}^{′}+9y & =0 \\\\ ({u}^{″}{e}^{3x}+6{u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+9u{e}^{3x})-6({u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+3u{e}^{3x})+9u{e}^{3x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{3x}({u}^{″}+6{u}^{′}+9u-6{u}^{′}-18u+9u) & =0 \\\\ {e}^{3x}{u}^{″} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $e^{3x} \\neq 0,$ we must have\n\n$$\nu'' = 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/example-6574.json" "file_sha256" "eca9c9e60be6c7ad4182da3210911b57effcb3382d3aa079eaa492a02835a915" "basis" 1987 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-6573" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $y_1(t) = e^{-t}$ is a solution to the equation $y'' - 3y ' - 4y = 0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution $y_2(t)$ to the equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/example-6573.json" "file_sha256" "6ae15ea9c8259dd0d16436bc0be70f6975a3cbbd1dc49866288363f78d7ea07e" "basis" 1987 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-6573" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to the reduction of order method, we let $y_{2} = u(t)e^{-t}.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & =u{e}^{-t} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{e}^{-t}-u{e}^{-t} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{e}^{-t}-2{u}^{′}{e}^{-t}+u{e}^{-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}-3{y}^{′}-4y & =0 \\\\ ({u}^{″}{e}^{-t}-2{u}^{′}{e}^{-t}+u{e}^{-t})-3({u}^{′}{e}^{-t}-u{e}^{-t})-4u{e}^{-t} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{e}^{-t}-5{u}^{′}{e}^{-t} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{-t}({u}^{″}-5{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $e^t \\neq 0,$ we must have\n\n$$\nu'' - 5u' = 0.\n$$\n\nLet $w = u'.$ Then our equation becomes a first-order equation,\n\n$$\nw' - 5w=0.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw = c_1 e^{5t}.\n$$\n\nNow, since since $w = u',$ we have\n\n$$\nu' = c_1e^{5t} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad u = \\dfrac{c_1}{5} e^{5t} + c_2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for $y_2(t) = u e^{-t}$ becomes\n\n$$\ny_2(t) = e^{-t}\\left( \\dfrac{c_1}{5} e^{5t} + c_2 \\right).\n$$\n\nWe can choose any values for $c_1$ and $c_2$ to yield a second solution. For example, if we set $c_1 = 5$ and $c_2 = 0,$ we get\n\n$$\ny_2(t) = e^{-t} \\cdot e^{5t} = e^{4t}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/example-6573.json" "file_sha256" "6ae15ea9c8259dd0d16436bc0be70f6975a3cbbd1dc49866288363f78d7ea07e" "basis" 1987 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14062296/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-102797" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{3}$ is a solution to the equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-6y=0$ for $x>0.$ If ${y}_{2}=u(x){y}_{1}(x)$ is a second solution of the equation, then which of the following equations must be satisfied by $u(x)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084856/state.json" "file_sha256" "556c9f350f3663d066c47f7dc4846f6a4df7f17820f2f299272833951412e45a"} {"question" "q-102797" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're given that\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=u(x){y}_{1}(x)=u{x}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating ${y}_{2}=u{x}^{3}$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{x}^{3}+3u{x}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{x}^{3}+6{u}^{′}{x}^{2}+6ux.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}({u}^{″}{x}^{3}+6{u}^{′}{x}^{2}+6ux)-6u{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{x}^{5}+6{u}^{′}{x}^{4}+6u{x}^{3}-6u{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{x}^{5}+6{u}^{′}{x}^{4} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{4}({u}^{″}x+6{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}^{4}\\ne 0$ for $x>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\nx{u}^{″}+6{u}^{′}=0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084856/state.json" "file_sha256" "556c9f350f3663d066c47f7dc4846f6a4df7f17820f2f299272833951412e45a"} {"question" "q-102797" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084856/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1e95b653b004c0f1b5b3635b9b863e6a6fa77ed99d91f68d9c468d2fcec327c1"} {"question" "q-102797" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084856/state.json" "file_sha256" "556c9f350f3663d066c47f7dc4846f6a4df7f17820f2f299272833951412e45a"} {"question" "q-102797" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x{u}^{″}+3{u}^{′}=0"] ["math" "x{u}^{″}+6{u}^{′}=0"] ["math" "{u}^{″}+6x{u}^{′}=0"] ["math" "{u}^{″}+6{u}^{′}=0"] ["math" "{x}^{2}{u}^{″}+3{u}^{′}=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084856/state.json" "file_sha256" "556c9f350f3663d066c47f7dc4846f6a4df7f17820f2f299272833951412e45a"} {"question" "q-102797" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x{u}^{″}+6{u}^{′}=0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084856/state.json" "file_sha256" "556c9f350f3663d066c47f7dc4846f6a4df7f17820f2f299272833951412e45a"} {"question" "q-102791" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{2x}$ is a solution to the equation ${y}^{″}-4{y}^{′}+4y=0.$ If ${y}_{2}=u(x){y}_{1}(x)$ is a second solution of the equation, then which of the following equations must be satisfied by $u(x)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084856/state.json" "file_sha256" "556c9f350f3663d066c47f7dc4846f6a4df7f17820f2f299272833951412e45a"} {"question" "q-102791" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're given that\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=u(x){y}_{1}(x)=u{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating ${y}_{2}=u{e}^{2x}$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{e}^{2x}+2u{e}^{2x} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{e}^{2x}+4{u}^{′}{e}^{2x}+4u{e}^{2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({u}^{″}{e}^{2x}+4{u}^{′}{e}^{2x}+4u{e}^{2x})-4({u}^{′}{e}^{2x}+2u{e}^{2x})+4u{e}^{2x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{2x}({u}^{″}+4{u}^{′}+4u-4{u}^{′}-8u+4u) & =0 \\\\ {e}^{2x}{u}^{″} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{2x}\\ne 0,$  we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}=0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084856/state.json" "file_sha256" "556c9f350f3663d066c47f7dc4846f6a4df7f17820f2f299272833951412e45a"} {"question" "q-102791" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084856/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1e95b653b004c0f1b5b3635b9b863e6a6fa77ed99d91f68d9c468d2fcec327c1"} {"question" "q-102791" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084856/state.json" "file_sha256" "556c9f350f3663d066c47f7dc4846f6a4df7f17820f2f299272833951412e45a"} {"question" "q-102791" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{u}^{″}+4u=0"] ["math" "{u}^{″}+8u=0"] ["math" "{u}^{″}-4xu=0"] ["math" "{u}^{″}=0"] ["math" "{u}^{″}={e}^{-2x}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084856/state.json" "file_sha256" "556c9f350f3663d066c47f7dc4846f6a4df7f17820f2f299272833951412e45a"} {"question" "q-102791" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{u}^{″}=0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084856/state.json" "file_sha256" "556c9f350f3663d066c47f7dc4846f6a4df7f17820f2f299272833951412e45a"} {"question" "q-64993" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function ${y}_{1}(x)={e}^{-4x}$ is a solution to the equation ${y}^{″}+8{y}^{′}+16y=0.$ If ${y}_{2}=u(x){y}_{1}(x)$ is a second solution of the equation, then which of the following equations must be satisfied by $u(x)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084856/state.json" "file_sha256" "556c9f350f3663d066c47f7dc4846f6a4df7f17820f2f299272833951412e45a"} {"question" "q-64993" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're given that\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=u(x){y}_{1}(x)=u{e}^{-4x}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating ${y}_{2}=u{e}^{-4x}$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{e}^{-4x}-4u{e}^{-4x} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{e}^{-4x}-8{u}^{′}{e}^{-4x}+16u{e}^{-4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({u}^{″}{e}^{-4x}-8{u}^{′}{e}^{-4x}+16u{e}^{-4x})+8({u}^{′}{e}^{-4x}-4u{e}^{-4x})+16u{e}^{-4x} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{e}^{-4x} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{-4x}\\ne 0$ for all $x,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}=0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084856/state.json" "file_sha256" "556c9f350f3663d066c47f7dc4846f6a4df7f17820f2f299272833951412e45a"} {"question" "q-64993" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084856/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1e95b653b004c0f1b5b3635b9b863e6a6fa77ed99d91f68d9c468d2fcec327c1"} {"question" "q-64993" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084856/state.json" "file_sha256" "556c9f350f3663d066c47f7dc4846f6a4df7f17820f2f299272833951412e45a"} {"question" "q-64993" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{u}^{″}+2{u}^{′}=0"] ["math" "{u}^{″}+8{u}^{′}=0"] ["math" "{u}^{″}-16{u}^{′}=0"] ["math" "{u}^{″}-4{u}^{′}=0"] ["math" "{u}^{″}=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084856/state.json" "file_sha256" "556c9f350f3663d066c47f7dc4846f6a4df7f17820f2f299272833951412e45a"} {"question" "q-64993" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{u}^{″}=0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084856/state.json" "file_sha256" "556c9f350f3663d066c47f7dc4846f6a4df7f17820f2f299272833951412e45a"} {"question" "q-90524" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function ${y}_{1}(t)={t}^{3}$ is a solution to the equation ${t}^{2}{y}^{″}-3t{y}^{′}+3y=0$ for $t>0.$ If ${y}_{2}=u(t){y}_{1}(t)$ is a second solution of the equation, then which of the following equations must be satisfied by $u(t)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084856/state.json" "file_sha256" "556c9f350f3663d066c47f7dc4846f6a4df7f17820f2f299272833951412e45a"} {"question" "q-90524" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're given that\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)=u(t){y}_{1}(t)=u{t}^{3}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating ${y}_{2}=u{t}^{3}$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{t}^{3}+3u{t}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{t}^{3}+6{u}^{′}{t}^{2}+6ut.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}({u}^{″}{t}^{3}+6{u}^{′}{t}^{2}+6ut)-3t({u}^{′}{t}^{3}+3u{t}^{2})+3u{t}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {t}^{3}({u}^{″}{t}^{2}+6{u}^{′}t+6u-3{u}^{′}t-9u+3u) & =0 \\\\ {t}^{3}({u}^{″}{t}^{2}+3{u}^{′}t) & =0 \\\\ {t}^{4}({u}^{″}t+3{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${t}^{4}\\ne 0$ for $t>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\nt{u}^{″}+3{u}^{′}=0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084856/state.json" "file_sha256" "556c9f350f3663d066c47f7dc4846f6a4df7f17820f2f299272833951412e45a"} {"question" "q-90524" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084856/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1e95b653b004c0f1b5b3635b9b863e6a6fa77ed99d91f68d9c468d2fcec327c1"} {"question" "q-90524" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084856/state.json" "file_sha256" "556c9f350f3663d066c47f7dc4846f6a4df7f17820f2f299272833951412e45a"} {"question" "q-90524" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "3{u}^{″}-{t}^{3}u=0"] ["math" "4{u}^{″}+t{u}^{′}=0"] ["math" "t{u}^{″}+3{u}^{′}=0"] ["math" "{t}^{3}{u}^{″}-{u}^{′}=0"] ["math" "{u}^{″}+2{t}^{3}u=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084856/state.json" "file_sha256" "556c9f350f3663d066c47f7dc4846f6a4df7f17820f2f299272833951412e45a"} {"question" "q-90524" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "t{u}^{″}+3{u}^{′}=0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084856/state.json" "file_sha256" "556c9f350f3663d066c47f7dc4846f6a4df7f17820f2f299272833951412e45a"} {"question" "q-64989" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function ${y}_{1}(t)={e}^{2t}$ is a solution to the equation ${y}^{″}+{y}^{′}-6y=0.$ If ${y}_{2}=u(t){y}_{1}(t)$ is a second solution of the equation, then which of the following equations must be satisfied by $u(t)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084856/state.json" "file_sha256" "556c9f350f3663d066c47f7dc4846f6a4df7f17820f2f299272833951412e45a"} {"question" "q-64989" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're given that\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)=u(t){y}_{1}(t)=u{e}^{2t}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating ${y}_{2}=u{e}^{2t}$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{e}^{2t}+2u{e}^{2t} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{e}^{2t}+4{u}^{′}{e}^{2t}+4u{e}^{2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({u}^{″}{e}^{2t}+4{u}^{′}{e}^{2t}+4u{e}^{2t})+({u}^{′}{e}^{2t}+2u{e}^{2t})-6u{e}^{2t} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{e}^{2t}+5{u}^{′}{e}^{2t} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{2t}({u}^{″}+5{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${e}^{2t}\\ne 0$ for all $t,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}+5{u}^{′}=0.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084856/state.json" "file_sha256" "556c9f350f3663d066c47f7dc4846f6a4df7f17820f2f299272833951412e45a"} {"question" "q-64989" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084856/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1e95b653b004c0f1b5b3635b9b863e6a6fa77ed99d91f68d9c468d2fcec327c1"} {"question" "q-64989" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084856/state.json" "file_sha256" "556c9f350f3663d066c47f7dc4846f6a4df7f17820f2f299272833951412e45a"} {"question" "q-64989" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2{u}^{″}+{u}^{′}=0"] ["math" "{e}^{2t}{u}^{″}+{u}^{′}=0"] ["math" "{u}^{″}+5{u}^{′}=0"] ["math" "{u}^{″}-{e}^{2t}{u}^{′}=0"] ["math" "{u}^{″}-{u}^{′}=0"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084856/state.json" "file_sha256" "556c9f350f3663d066c47f7dc4846f6a4df7f17820f2f299272833951412e45a"} {"question" "q-64989" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{u}^{″}+5{u}^{′}=0"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084856/state.json" "file_sha256" "556c9f350f3663d066c47f7dc4846f6a4df7f17820f2f299272833951412e45a"} {"question" "q-64958" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{3}$ is a solution to the equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-x{y}^{′}-3y=0$ for $x>0.$ Use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(x)$ to the equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084856/state.json" "file_sha256" "556c9f350f3663d066c47f7dc4846f6a4df7f17820f2f299272833951412e45a"} {"question" "q-64958" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x)\\cdot {x}^{3}.$ \n\nDifferentiating ${y}_{2}=u(x){x}^{3}$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{x}^{3}+3u{x}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{x}^{3}+6{u}^{′}{x}^{2}+6ux.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}({u}^{″}{x}^{3}+6{u}^{′}{x}^{2}+6ux)-x({u}^{′}{x}^{3}+3u{x}^{2})-3u{x}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}({u}^{″}{x}^{2}+6{u}^{′}x+6u-{u}^{′}x-3u-3u) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{4}({u}^{″}x+5{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}^{4}\\ne 0$ for $x>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}x+5{u}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}x+5w=0.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}{x}^{-5}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}{x}^{-5}\\,⟹\\,u=-\\frac{1}{4}{c}_{1}{x}^{-4}+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u{x}^{3}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{3}(-\\frac{1}{4}{c}_{1}{x}^{-4}+{c}_{2}).\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${c}_{1}=-4$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=\\frac{1}{x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084856/state.json" "file_sha256" "556c9f350f3663d066c47f7dc4846f6a4df7f17820f2f299272833951412e45a"} {"question" "q-64958" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084856/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1e95b653b004c0f1b5b3635b9b863e6a6fa77ed99d91f68d9c468d2fcec327c1"} {"question" "q-64958" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084856/state.json" "file_sha256" "556c9f350f3663d066c47f7dc4846f6a4df7f17820f2f299272833951412e45a"} {"question" "q-64958" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{y}_{2}(x)=\\frac{1}{x}"] ["math" "{y}_{2}(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}"] ["math" "{y}_{2}(x)=x"] ["math" "{y}_{2}(x)={e}^{x}"] ["math" "{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084856/state.json" "file_sha256" "556c9f350f3663d066c47f7dc4846f6a4df7f17820f2f299272833951412e45a"} {"question" "q-64958" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}_{2}(x)=\\frac{1}{x}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084856/state.json" "file_sha256" "556c9f350f3663d066c47f7dc4846f6a4df7f17820f2f299272833951412e45a"} {"question" "q-77666" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function ${y}_{1}(t)={t}^{3}$ is a solution to the equation ${t}^{2}{y}^{″}-5t{y}^{′}+9y=0$ for $t>0.$ Use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(t)$ to the equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084856/state.json" "file_sha256" "556c9f350f3663d066c47f7dc4846f6a4df7f17820f2f299272833951412e45a"} {"question" "q-77666" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(t)\\cdot {t}^{3}.$ \n\nDifferentiating ${y}_{2}=u(t){t}^{3}$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{t}^{3}+3u{t}^{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{t}^{3}+6{u}^{′}{t}^{2}+6ut.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}({u}^{″}{t}^{3}+6{u}^{′}{t}^{2}+6ut)-5t({u}^{′}{t}^{3}+3u{t}^{2})+9u{t}^{3} & =0 \\\\ {t}^{3}({u}^{″}{t}^{2}+6{u}^{′}t+6u-5{u}^{′}t-15u+9u) & =0 \\\\ {t}^{3}({u}^{″}{t}^{2}+{u}^{′}t) & =0 \\\\ {t}^{4}({u}^{″}t+{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${t}^{4}\\ne 0$ for $t>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}t+{u}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}t+w=0.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{t}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{t}\\,⟹\\,u={c}_{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(t)=u{t}^{3}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)={t}^{3}({c}_{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+{c}_{2}).\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)={t}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084856/state.json" "file_sha256" "556c9f350f3663d066c47f7dc4846f6a4df7f17820f2f299272833951412e45a"} {"question" "q-77666" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084856/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1e95b653b004c0f1b5b3635b9b863e6a6fa77ed99d91f68d9c468d2fcec327c1"} {"question" "q-77666" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084856/state.json" "file_sha256" "556c9f350f3663d066c47f7dc4846f6a4df7f17820f2f299272833951412e45a"} {"question" "q-77666" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{y}_{2}(t)=\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}"] ["math" "{y}_{2}(t)=\\frac{{t}^{3}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡t}"] ["math" "{y}_{2}(t)=t{ln}^{2}⁡t"] ["math" "{y}_{2}(t)={t}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t"] ["math" "{y}_{2}(t)={t}^{5}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084856/state.json" "file_sha256" "556c9f350f3663d066c47f7dc4846f6a4df7f17820f2f299272833951412e45a"} {"question" "q-77666" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}_{2}(t)={t}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084856/state.json" "file_sha256" "556c9f350f3663d066c47f7dc4846f6a4df7f17820f2f299272833951412e45a"} {"question" "q-65020" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function ${y}_{1}(t)={t}^{2}$ is a solution to the equation ${t}^{2}{y}^{″}-3t{y}^{′}+4y=0$ for $t>0.$ Use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(t)$ to the equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084856/state.json" "file_sha256" "556c9f350f3663d066c47f7dc4846f6a4df7f17820f2f299272833951412e45a"} {"question" "q-65020" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(t)\\cdot {t}^{2}.$ \n\nDifferentiating ${y}_{2}=u(t){t}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{t}^{2}+2ut \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{t}^{2}+4{u}^{′}t+2u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2}({u}^{″}{t}^{2}+4{u}^{′}t+2u)-3t({u}^{′}{t}^{2}+2ut)+4u{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {t}^{2}({u}^{″}{t}^{2}+4{u}^{′}t+2u-3{u}^{′}t-6u+4u) & =0 \\\\ {t}^{2}({u}^{″}{t}^{2}+{u}^{′}t) & =0 \\\\ {t}^{3}({u}^{″}t+{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${t}^{3}\\ne 0$ for $t>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}t+{u}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}t+w=0.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{t}.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{t}\\,⟹\\,u={c}_{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(t)=u{t}^{2}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)={t}^{2}({c}_{1}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+{c}_{2}).\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${c}_{1}=1$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(t)={t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084856/state.json" "file_sha256" "556c9f350f3663d066c47f7dc4846f6a4df7f17820f2f299272833951412e45a"} {"question" "q-65020" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084856/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1e95b653b004c0f1b5b3635b9b863e6a6fa77ed99d91f68d9c468d2fcec327c1"} {"question" "q-65020" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084856/state.json" "file_sha256" "556c9f350f3663d066c47f7dc4846f6a4df7f17820f2f299272833951412e45a"} {"question" "q-65020" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{y}_{2}(t)=\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}"] ["math" "{y}_{2}(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t"] ["math" "{y}_{2}(t)=t"] ["math" "{y}_{2}(t)=t\\operatorname{ln}⁡t"] ["math" "{y}_{2}(t)={t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084856/state.json" "file_sha256" "556c9f350f3663d066c47f7dc4846f6a4df7f17820f2f299272833951412e45a"} {"question" "q-65020" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}_{2}(t)={t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084856/state.json" "file_sha256" "556c9f350f3663d066c47f7dc4846f6a4df7f17820f2f299272833951412e45a"} {"question" "q-65000" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{2}$ is a solution to the equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{″}-5x{y}^{′}+8y=0$ for $x>0.$ Use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(x)$ to the equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084856/state.json" "file_sha256" "556c9f350f3663d066c47f7dc4846f6a4df7f17820f2f299272833951412e45a"} {"question" "q-65000" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x)\\cdot {x}^{2}.$ \n\nDifferentiating ${y}_{2}=u(x){x}^{2}$ with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{x}^{2}+2ux \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{x}^{2}+4{u}^{′}x+2u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}({u}^{″}{x}^{2}+4{u}^{′}x+2u)-5x({u}^{′}{x}^{2}+2ux)+8u{x}^{2} & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}({u}^{″}{x}^{2}+4{u}^{′}x+2u-5{u}^{′}x-10u+8u) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}({u}^{″}{x}^{2}-{u}^{′}x) & =0 \\\\ {x}^{3}({u}^{″}x-{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince ${x}^{3}\\ne 0$ for $x>0,$ we must have\n\n$$\n{u}^{″}x-{u}^{′}=0.\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}x-w=0.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}x.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}x\\,⟹\\,u=\\frac{{c}_{1}}{2}{x}^{2}+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u{x}^{2}$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{2}(\\frac{{c}_{1}}{2}{x}^{2}+{c}_{2}).\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${c}_{1}=2$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{2}={x}^{4}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084856/state.json" "file_sha256" "556c9f350f3663d066c47f7dc4846f6a4df7f17820f2f299272833951412e45a"} {"question" "q-65000" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084856/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1e95b653b004c0f1b5b3635b9b863e6a6fa77ed99d91f68d9c468d2fcec327c1"} {"question" "q-65000" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084856/state.json" "file_sha256" "556c9f350f3663d066c47f7dc4846f6a4df7f17820f2f299272833951412e45a"} {"question" "q-65000" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{y}_{2}(x)=\\frac{1}{x}"] ["math" "{y}_{2}(x)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x"] ["math" "{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x"] ["math" "{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{3}"] ["math" "{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{4}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084856/state.json" "file_sha256" "556c9f350f3663d066c47f7dc4846f6a4df7f17820f2f299272833951412e45a"} {"question" "q-65000" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}_{2}(x)={x}^{4}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084856/state.json" "file_sha256" "556c9f350f3663d066c47f7dc4846f6a4df7f17820f2f299272833951412e45a"} {"question" "q-64998" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x$ is a solution to the equation ${y}^{″}+9y=0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution ${y}_{2}(x)$ to the equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084856/state.json" "file_sha256" "556c9f350f3663d066c47f7dc4846f6a4df7f17820f2f299272833951412e45a"} {"question" "q-64998" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to the reduction of order method, we let ${y}_{2}=u(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+3u\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+6{u}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-9u\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({u}^{″}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+6{u}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x-9u\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x)+9u\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x+6{u}^{′}\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″} & =-6{u}^{′}\\operatorname{cot}⁡3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $w={u}^{′}.$ Then our equation becomes\n\n$$\n{w}^{′}=-6w\\operatorname{cot}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw={c}_{1}{csc}^{2}⁡3x.\n$$\n\nBut since $w={u}^{′},$ we have\n\n$$\n{u}^{′}={c}_{1}{csc}^{2}⁡3x\\,⟹\\,u=-\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3}\\operatorname{cot}⁡3x+{c}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for ${y}_{2}(x)=u\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x$ becomes\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x(-\\frac{{c}_{1}}{3}\\operatorname{cot}⁡3x+{c}_{2}).\n$$\n\nFinally, setting ${c}_{1}=-3$ and ${c}_{2}=0,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x(\\operatorname{cot}⁡3x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084856/state.json" "file_sha256" "556c9f350f3663d066c47f7dc4846f6a4df7f17820f2f299272833951412e45a"} {"question" "q-64998" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084856/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1e95b653b004c0f1b5b3635b9b863e6a6fa77ed99d91f68d9c468d2fcec327c1"} {"question" "q-64998" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084856/state.json" "file_sha256" "556c9f350f3663d066c47f7dc4846f6a4df7f17820f2f299272833951412e45a"} {"question" "q-64998" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x"] ["math" "{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{csc}⁡3x"] ["math" "{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡3x"] ["math" "{y}_{2}(x)=x\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x"] ["math" "{y}_{2}(x)=x\\operatorname{sin}⁡3x"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084856/state.json" "file_sha256" "556c9f350f3663d066c47f7dc4846f6a4df7f17820f2f299272833951412e45a"} {"question" "q-64998" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "{y}_{2}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡3x"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084856/state.json" "file_sha256" "556c9f350f3663d066c47f7dc4846f6a4df7f17820f2f299272833951412e45a"} {"question" "e-6574" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The function $y_1(x) = e^{3x}$ is a solution to the equation $y'' - 6y' + 9y = 0.$ If $y_{2} = u(x) y_1(x)$ is a second solution of the equation, then which of the following equations must be satisfied by $u(x)?$\n\n- $u''=0$\n\n- $e^{3x}u'' + u=0$\n\n- $u''-u=0$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084856/example-6574.json" "file_sha256" "eca9c9e60be6c7ad4182da3210911b57effcb3382d3aa079eaa492a02835a915"} {"question" "e-6574" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We're given that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}(x) & =u(x){y}_{1}(x) \\\\  & =u(x){e}^{3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $x$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & =u{e}^{3x} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+3u{e}^{3x} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{e}^{3x}+6{u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+9u{e}^{3x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}-6{y}^{′}+9y & =0 \\\\ ({u}^{″}{e}^{3x}+6{u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+9u{e}^{3x})-6({u}^{′}{e}^{3x}+3u{e}^{3x})+9u{e}^{3x} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{3x}({u}^{″}+6{u}^{′}+9u-6{u}^{′}-18u+9u) & =0 \\\\ {e}^{3x}{u}^{″} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $e^{3x} \\neq 0,$ we must have\n\n$$\nu'' = 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084856/example-6574.json" "file_sha256" "eca9c9e60be6c7ad4182da3210911b57effcb3382d3aa079eaa492a02835a915"} {"question" "e-6573" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $y_1(t) = e^{-t}$ is a solution to the equation $y'' - 3y ' - 4y = 0,$ use reduction of order to find a second solution $y_2(t)$ to the equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084856/example-6573.json" "file_sha256" "6ae15ea9c8259dd0d16436bc0be70f6975a3cbbd1dc49866288363f78d7ea07e"} {"question" "e-6573" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "According to the reduction of order method, we let $y_{2} = u(t)e^{-t}.$ \n\nDifferentiating the above with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2} & =u{e}^{-t} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{′} & ={u}^{′}{e}^{-t}-u{e}^{-t} \\\\ {y}_{2}^{″} & ={u}^{″}{e}^{-t}-2{u}^{′}{e}^{-t}+u{e}^{-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}-3{y}^{′}-4y & =0 \\\\ ({u}^{″}{e}^{-t}-2{u}^{′}{e}^{-t}+u{e}^{-t})-3({u}^{′}{e}^{-t}-u{e}^{-t})-4u{e}^{-t} & =0 \\\\ {u}^{″}{e}^{-t}-5{u}^{′}{e}^{-t} & =0 \\\\ {e}^{-t}({u}^{″}-5{u}^{′}) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $e^t \\neq 0,$ we must have\n\n$$\nu'' - 5u' = 0.\n$$\n\nLet $w = u'.$ Then our equation becomes a first-order equation,\n\n$$\nw' - 5w=0.\n$$\n\nThis is a separable equation that has the solution\n\n$$\nw = c_1 e^{5t}.\n$$\n\nNow, since since $w = u',$ we have\n\n$$\nu' = c_1e^{5t} \\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad u = \\dfrac{c_1}{5} e^{5t} + c_2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our expression for $y_2(t) = u e^{-t}$ becomes\n\n$$\ny_2(t) = e^{-t}\\left( \\dfrac{c_1}{5} e^{5t} + c_2 \\right).\n$$\n\nWe can choose any values for $c_1$ and $c_2$ to yield a second solution. For example, if we set $c_1 = 5$ and $c_2 = 0,$ we get\n\n$$\ny_2(t) = e^{-t} \\cdot e^{5t} = e^{4t}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14084856/example-6573.json" "file_sha256" "6ae15ea9c8259dd0d16436bc0be70f6975a3cbbd1dc49866288363f78d7ea07e"}] "usage" {"q-65000" [] "q-64958" [] "e-6573" [] "q-64998" [] "e-6574" []} "basis" 2885 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [17592186123611 17592186123612 17592186123613 17592186123614 17592186123615 17592186342546 17592186342547 17592186342548 17592186342549 17592186342550 17592186342553 17592186342554 17592186342555 17592186342556 17592186342557] "immutable_fields" [[17592186123610 :answer-field/correct] [17592186123610 :answer-field/id] [17592186123610 :answer-field/key] [17592186123610 :answer-field/type] [17592186342545 :answer-field/correct] [17592186342545 :answer-field/id] [17592186342545 :answer-field/key] [17592186342545 :answer-field/type] [17592186342552 :answer-field/correct] [17592186342552 :answer-field/id] [17592186342552 :answer-field/key] [17592186342552 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
